00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.030
You mentioned SPDY,
you mentioned HTTP/2,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.450
you mentioned QUIC.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.070
Why don't we start
with the first one?

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.389
So what was SPDY, what was that?

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:40.180
And we can probably
continue on from there.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.013
ILYA GRIGORIK: Sure.

00:03:41.013 --> 00:03:45.450
So when we started working
on Chrome within Google,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.240
one of the things that
we realized very early on

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.260
is that the current web
pages that we're building

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:58.690
are much more complicated
than what the HP protocol was

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.230
originally designed for.

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:02.840
So the protocol that the
web is built on today

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.480
was more or less standardized
10 years ago, even actually

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.500
more so back in 1999.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.410
And since then, we've started
building much more complicated

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:12.618
and interesting applications.

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:14.420
We went from pages
to applications

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.690
that fetch hundreds of
resources and all the rest.

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:20.010
So when the effort to
start working on Chrome

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.474
kicked off in around 2007,
2008, we very quickly

00:04:23.474 --> 00:04:24.890
realized that,
first of all, speed

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.320
is one of the tenets of Chrome.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.750
We want to focus on speed.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.790
And we identified HTTP as one
of the primary bottlenecks,

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:33.290
where just the
number of connections

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:35.224
that you had to manage,
it's inefficient

00:04:35.224 --> 00:04:36.640
both on the server
and the client.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.130
And we were really
limited in the parallelism

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.724
for how many resources we could
fetch, what kind of signals

00:04:43.724 --> 00:04:45.390
we can communicate
to the server to help

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.760
prioritize how the data is
returned, and all the rest.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:54.590
So that was the genesis of SPDY,
where we took us aback and just

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.270
looked at what could
we do to HTTP to help

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.280
reduce or eliminate
some of the bottlenecks.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.800
So the basic
insights were, well,

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:09.050
if we add things like framing,
such that we can split messages

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.760
instead of having
one message occupy

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.980
the entire connection, which is
how the HTTP/1 protocol works,

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.540
where you send the
request, you have

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.040
to wait for the entire
response before you

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:19.123
can send the next request.

00:05:19.123 --> 00:05:22.460
What if we could actually send
multiple requests and responses

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.250
over the same connection?

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.055
So that framing.

00:05:25.055 --> 00:05:27.180
Then we looked at, well,
what about prioritization?

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.150
So it turns out that
modern applications

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:32.840
are both smart and complicated.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.630
Things like, as you
start scrolling,

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.180
it would be nice if
we could reprioritize

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.757
things-- like, say, an image
scrolls out of the uPort.

00:05:39.757 --> 00:05:41.590
It'd be nice to let to
server know that hey,

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.690
this is maybe not as
important as it was before.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.190
This is no longer visible.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.220
Or even simpler
things, like, well, I

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.040
need this JavaScript file before
this image file because it's

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.070
blocking rendering.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.130
That's not something you could
actually express in a protocol

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:53.629
before.

00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:56.400
So we started looking
at priorities.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.006
And then there's
another feature that

00:06:00.006 --> 00:06:01.130
has to do with compression.

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:06.349
So we realized that
the headers of HTTP

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:07.890
are always transferred
in plain text.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:08.848
They're not compressed.

00:06:08.848 --> 00:06:10.860
HTTP allows the bodies
to be compressed.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.450
But the GET header is
the user agent's string,

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:16.430
and all those things are always
transferred uncompressed.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.910
And frankly, they're kind
of redundant oftentimes.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:22.350
And there's a lot of that
data going back and forth.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.810
So if we could
compress that data,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.490
that would also speed
up the protocol.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:29.351
And then building
on those primitives,

00:06:29.351 --> 00:06:31.600
there's additional things
that went into the protocols

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.891
to just make it more efficient.

00:06:32.891 --> 00:06:34.900
And that was effectively
the genesis of SPDY.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:35.920
The Chrome team
implemented that,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.450
or started
experimenting with it.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:39.820
They started implementing
it also on the server.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.986
So our Google servers in Chrome
were some of the first ones

00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:47.770
to test this out and gather some
data from out the wild to see,

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.920
does this work?

00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:51.480
And lo and behold, it
turns out that it did work.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.550
It actually helped some of
our services quite a bit.

00:06:54.550 --> 00:07:00.520
So we took that proposal to
IETF and started the discussion,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.100
around what can we
do in the space?

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:07.160
SPDY is just one way of
tackling this problem.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.170
And after some back
and forth, the IETF

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:13.120
started a new effort
to define HTTP/2,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.130
and actually adopted
SPDY, which at the time

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.950
was draft to a version
too of the protocol

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.760
as a starting point.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.200
And then the working
group took over,

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.610
and we continued to contribute
to it and work with the team

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:27.080
there.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.092
And that effectively
became HTTP/2.

00:07:29.092 --> 00:07:31.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:34.820
So what you're saying is
that at the beginning,

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:39.070
HTTP/2 was basically SPDY, which
was a Google product-- a Google

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.450
project., sorry.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:45.320
But from there it kept on
evolving to become a standard.

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:49.510
What are the main differences
between SPDY and HTTP/2?

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.302
What was added or
removed from it?

00:07:51.302 --> 00:07:53.510
ILYA GRIGORIK: So there's
a lot of low level details,

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:55.850
like the framings is
slightly different.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.600
If you care about how the
bits are aligned on the wire,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.620
there's quite a
few subtle changes.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:02.410
We change the definitions
on the frames.

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:04.920
We changed how the
prioritization scheme works.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.530
So for example, in SPDY, we
had a fairly simple model where

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.230
you could just assign a weight.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.870
In HTTP/2, it's much
more comprehensive.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.600
It allows you to express
both weights and dependencies

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.470
between resources.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.740
We've done some additional
work on server push,

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.906
which is something we
actually haven't talked about.

00:08:20.906 --> 00:08:22.770
But in a HTTP/2, we
have this new capability

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.750
where, in response
to a single request,

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:29.010
the server can actually push
multiple responses back.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:31.610
So a use case here would
be, hey, you came and asked

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:33.840
for the index.html.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.409
But I know that you're going
to ask for the JavaScript

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:38.690
file and the style sheet.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:41.336
So why should I wait for
another round trip for you

00:08:41.336 --> 00:08:43.419
to just come back to me
and ask me for this thing?

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:43.950
So here.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:44.770
Just have it.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.220
So that's server push.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:49.600
And there's some
other kind of cleanups

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.170
in all the rest that was added.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:55.590
So the HTTP/2
draft, or that work,

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:59.080
I think it went through--
I don't want to lie,

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.460
but it was definitely more
than a dozen iterations.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.170
And it was actually
really, really good

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.750
because unlike a lot
of the other protocols,

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:12.120
this was being developed
and tested in the field.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.490
So very early on, of course,
we had Chrome support.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:17.180
Firefox also supported SPDY.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:20.130
IE and Safari also
jumped on board.

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:24.880
So while we were testing
HTTP/2, we were actually very,

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:25.680
very data driven.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.999
So we had these meetings
every three or four months

00:09:28.999 --> 00:09:31.040
where we would come back
and do interrupt testing

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.960
between all the browsers
and the major servers.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.376
Of course, there
was Google, there

00:09:34.376 --> 00:09:36.964
was Twitter, there was
Facebook, and others

00:09:36.964 --> 00:09:38.630
that were experimenting
with this stuff.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:42.727
So we could make
hypotheses and say, hey,

00:09:42.727 --> 00:09:44.310
we would like to
propose this feature.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:45.680
Instead of just
arguing about it,

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:46.950
we could actually
try and implement it,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.660
see what the data says, and then
come back to the drawing board

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:51.320
and say what worked
and what didn't.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:55.140
So by the time the HTTP/2
spec was actually finalized

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.030
and went out as a
standard, we already

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.150
had implementations
in all the browsers.

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.840
We had very good
implementations in many servers,

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:04.260
including open source versions.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:07.820
So it was probably one of
the best tested protocols

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.640
that we've released
in a long time

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.167
amongst all the
different stacks.

00:10:12.167 --> 00:10:13.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:14.450
So from the
beginning, we already

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:19.880
had support for SPDY and
HTTP/2 on some Google services

00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:20.780
and Chrome.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.830
And other people
also jumped into it.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:26.050
When did we start having
that kind of support

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:28.400
in other Google services,
like specially Google Cloud

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:28.967
Platform?

00:10:28.967 --> 00:10:30.550
ILYA GRIGORIK: That's
a good question.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.600
I don't know the exact date.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:37.300
But I can say that, of
course most, if not all,

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.142
of our services at
Google, actually

00:10:39.142 --> 00:10:40.850
are served by more or
less the same front

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.260
end, which is our Google
Front End-- or GFE, as we

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:44.600
call it an internally.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:48.360
And the GFE was where we've
implemented SPDY support.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.820
So the support for
SPDY was effectively

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:54.580
enabled for any and
all Google service,

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:55.890
as long as they use HTTPS.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:57.590
This is actually one thing
we haven't talked about.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.210
But in order to
use SPDY in HTTP/2,

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:02.120
you have to use HTTPS
because the negotiation

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:06.490
for the protocol happens when
the TLS handshake is done.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:09.360
That's how the client advertises
that, hey, I support HTTP/2,

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:12.530
and the server picks that.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:18.990
And I believe, actually, that
we had silent support for SPDY

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.980
for a very long time,
especially in places

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.360
like App Engine,
where we actually

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.690
haven't advertised it much.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:28.470
But we were certainly the first
platform that supported it.

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:33.240
So if you happen to run your
service on top of App Engine

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.260
and with HTTPS, you probably
didn't even know it,

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.156
but you were already
SPDY-enabled,

00:11:38.156 --> 00:11:39.030
which is pretty cool.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:40.488
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So one more reason

00:11:40.488 --> 00:11:43.671
to use HTTPS on top of security
is that it will be even faster.

00:11:43.671 --> 00:11:44.170
That's nice.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:45.040
MARK: It will be even faster.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:45.550
So OK.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:46.970
So you're extending
on from there.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.182
It leads us quite nicely into--
so people are saying, yep,

00:11:50.182 --> 00:11:50.890
HTTP/2's awesome.

00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.020
I want the extra speed.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.270
You know it's going to
make my sights faster.

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.770
How do I get HTTP/2 now on
like Google Cloud Platform?

00:11:57.770 --> 00:11:59.835
What do I need to do
to get that enabled?

00:11:59.835 --> 00:12:00.710
ILYA GRIGORIK: Right.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.440
So actually, I think that's
an even better question for me

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:06.026
to phrase to you guys.

00:12:06.026 --> 00:12:08.090
You know it probably
even better than I do.

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:11.460
But depending on
how you set it up,

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:13.200
you need to make sure
that, first of all,

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.560
we said HTTPS, right?

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.951
That's point one.

00:12:16.951 --> 00:12:18.700
Once you have HTTPS,
you need to make sure

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:21.820
that whatever you're
using is able to negotiate

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:24.220
HTTP/2 on your behalf.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:27.161
And then this question of how's
that routed to your back end?

00:12:27.161 --> 00:12:29.410
So if you're using something
like a TCP load balancer,

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:32.340
then you have to
terminate the HTTPS

00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.960
and have a server that is
capable of talking HTTP/2.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.660
Whereas if your using
the HTTPS load balancer,

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:41.920
which is HTTP aware,
then it will do that work

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:43.370
on your behalf.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:47.810
And I think we have a
pretty nice diagram--

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:48.310
MARK: We do.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:50.640
ILYA GRIGORIK: --on our blog
that that shows all of that.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.200
MARK: That might have been a
leading question considering

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:53.850
that you and I worked
on that blog together.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:54.516
But that's fine.

00:12:54.516 --> 00:12:55.467
I'm happy with that.

00:12:55.467 --> 00:12:56.300
That's totally fine.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:56.940
So yeah.

00:12:56.940 --> 00:12:57.670
It's pretty cool.

00:12:57.670 --> 00:13:00.270
You put a load balancer in
front of it, you go HTTPS,

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:04.430
you get HTTP/2 support, you
use App Engine, you use HTTPS,

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:05.760
you get HTTP/2 support.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:06.770
I really like this.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.630
I think it's actually really,
really, really powerful.

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:08.850
ILYA GRIGORIK: Yeah.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.400
So actually, I think
this highlights something

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:11.080
very important.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:15.190
So SPDY and HTTP/2 do not
change anything semantically

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:16.482
about how HTTP works.

00:13:16.482 --> 00:13:18.690
It's all the same methods,
it's all the same headers,

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:19.670
it's all the rest.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:21.850
So when I said
earlier that if you're

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.690
running on HTTPS
on an app engine,

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:26.930
you're probably running SPDY
and you didn't even know it,

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:28.580
that's very important.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:31.980
So even today you're thinking,
so how do I enable HTTP/2?

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.630
It's like, well,
switch over to HTTPS.

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:39.330
Put a load balancer on a server
that is able to speak HTTP/2.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:40.780
And your application will work.

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:42.590
There is nothing extra
that you need to do.

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:45.140
Now, there are things
that you could perhaps

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.970
do within your
application to take

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:49.570
better advantage of some of the
things that HTTP/2 provides.

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:52.340
But that's a whole
different matter.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:52.840
MARK: No.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:53.381
That's great.

00:13:53.381 --> 00:13:54.922
That's great.

00:13:54.922 --> 00:13:56.380
So if they want to
take advantage--

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.175
we talked a little
bit about server push,

00:13:58.175 --> 00:13:59.447
like pushing up assets.

00:13:59.447 --> 00:14:01.030
Are there any other
things that people

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.015
might be able to take advantage
of they might want to?

00:14:04.015 --> 00:14:05.390
ILYA GRIGORIK:
Well, I think this

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:08.950
is where we get back or get
into what kind of things

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:12.300
you can do to get the
most out of HTTP/2.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:17.270
So HTTP/2 removes a lot of the
bottlenecks that we previously

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:20.090
had to work around, things
like concatenated files.

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:23.470
And for the most part, those
primarily because the requests,

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:25.310
having multiple
independent requests

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:28.170
was just very costly from
a latency perspective.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:31.330
So we would take all of our like
beautiful modular JavaScripts

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:35.630
and CSS and put it into
one file and say, here.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:37.000
Here's a workaround.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.820
Just fetch this one big
blob of data, which is fine.

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:42.469
It works, except that
it does run into issues

00:14:42.469 --> 00:14:43.510
with things like caching.

00:14:43.510 --> 00:14:46.050
So now your designer comes
along, changes one color,

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:48.050
and then all of a sudden
your entire style sheet

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.060
is invalidated.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.890
Could you have split that
such that the things that

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:57.800
are changing rapidly are
small, such that next time it's

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.450
updated you just
update that one file?

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:01.010
That's fewer bytes
for the client

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.980
to fetch when they
come back to your site.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.690
It also means that the
content is fetched faster.

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:09.129
So it's less load, it's faster
load, it's a win all around.

00:15:09.129 --> 00:15:11.670
So now you can actually start
thinking about that and saying,

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:15.610
well, I probably already put in
some automation in place that

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.680
puts all the files
together into one bundle.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.252
Maybe I want to
undo all of that.

00:15:19.252 --> 00:15:21.210
I probably don't want to
ship hundreds of files

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.830
because that has
its own limitations.

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:27.580
But dozens is probably a
very good kind of safe spot.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:29.970
So that's one very
concrete example.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.320
The other one is server
push, as you mentioned.

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.195
So this is actually
something that developers

00:15:34.195 --> 00:15:36.040
have been using unknowingly.

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.410
This is very
similar to inlining.

00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:41.540
So with inlining, it's kind
of a similar hack that says,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:45.166
well, maybe the
request is so small,

00:15:45.166 --> 00:15:46.540
or I don't want
you to come back.

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.590
So I'm just going to embed
it in another resource.

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:49.800
I'm going to take
this image, and just

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.850
basic C foreign code it,
and put into HTML such

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.540
that you don't even
have to ask me for it.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:57.910
But that's what server
push accomplishes well,

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:01.770
except server push, when
the resource is pushed,

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.220
it actually goes into the cache.

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:04.740
So it's separate
from the HTML file.

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.010
So that resource can be
reused across multiple files,

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:09.194
it can be invalidated,
all the same things.

00:16:09.194 --> 00:16:12.090
So that's another example.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:15.100
And for server push,
I think we have

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:17.220
some exciting news coming up.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:20.984
Maybe once this
podcast goes live,

00:16:20.984 --> 00:16:22.400
that will show you
how to use that

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:24.730
on App Engine and
other places as well.

00:16:24.730 --> 00:16:26.370
So I'm really
excited about that.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:28.245
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
sounds pretty exciting.

00:16:28.245 --> 00:16:31.530
So one of the things that
I had to deal before with

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.650
and I didn't really
enjoy was the fact

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:36.360
that to serve better
a bunch of images,

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.520
I had to put them
all in a single image

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.905
and then do CSS
tricks with that.

00:16:40.905 --> 00:16:44.715
Does that mean that with HTTP/2
that is not needed anymore?

00:16:44.715 --> 00:16:45.590
ILYA GRIGORIK: Right.

00:16:45.590 --> 00:16:48.830
So it's the exact same issue
as the JavaScript and CSS.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:50.864
We have lots of small icons.

00:16:50.864 --> 00:16:52.780
And it's just very
expensive to fetch them all

00:16:52.780 --> 00:16:56.350
because we have effectively--
the maximum parallelism is six

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:58.490
connections or six requests.

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:01.770
And my page just happens
to have 100 icons.

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:03.214
That's going to
take a long time.

00:17:03.214 --> 00:17:04.380
That's a lot of round trips.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:07.290
So we put them all into one
file and then use a CSS axe.

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:07.930
So yeah.

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:10.140
This is no longer necessary
because now the server,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.306
or the client rather, can
just send all 100 requests

00:17:12.306 --> 00:17:13.650
over the single connection.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.569
And then the server can respond
and interweave that data

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:17.140
as it wishes.

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:17.849
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is really awesome.

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:19.390
ILYA GRIGORIK: Now,
as I said before,

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:23.380
if you have thousands
of images, there

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:24.960
are other concerns
where it may still

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.579
be useful to do some
sort of spriting.

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:30.324
So for example, let's say all
your icons are kind of similar.

00:17:30.324 --> 00:17:32.240
You could actually get
much better compression

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:36.150
if you put some of them together
in terms of your actual image

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:37.460
format.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.650
So there's some interesting
areas to explore here.

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:43.490
It's not just like
unbundle everything.

00:17:43.490 --> 00:17:46.960
But at the same time, I think
that's an exception, not

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:47.640
the rule now.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:50.390
Whereas before, as a rule
we had to put everything

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:53.717
into one image file, or once
CSS file, or one JavaScript.

00:17:53.717 --> 00:17:55.300
And now that's more
of a nice pattern.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:57.250
MARK: It kind of changes
their rules of thumbs

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.620
that you would
necessarily apply when you

00:17:59.620 --> 00:18:01.130
started building this stuff.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:01.740
That makes a lot of sense.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:02.656
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:02.656 --> 00:18:06.270
This makes me think about the
demo that we'll put on the show

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:07.240
now for sure.

00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:13.820
It's this HTTP demo developed
by [INAUDIBLE] from the Go team.

00:18:13.820 --> 00:18:15.720
So basically, they
show the difference

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:18.270
between loading--
I think it's maybe

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:23.570
100 little images on
HTTP/1 versus HTTP/2.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.290
And it's quite amazing.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:27.717
The difference is
really night and day.

00:18:27.717 --> 00:18:28.550
ILYA GRIGORIK: Yeah.

00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:32.030
And it's really important that
as you look at those images,

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.590
you try and grok
as to why that is.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:36.620
And in HTTP/1
world, it's loading

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:40.880
slowly because we can fetch it,
at most six styles at a time.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.300
And then there's
many round trips

00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:44.130
to fetch all the
necessary tiles.

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:47.350
And with HTTP/2, the client just
sends all the requests at once.

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:50.100
So effectively, we're
limited by the speed

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:52.784
of the link between the
client and the server.

00:18:52.784 --> 00:18:53.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:55.800
So there's a lot of
benefits from moving

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:58.330
from HTTP/1 to HTTP/2.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:03.110
We actually came via first
that experiment that was SPDY.

00:19:03.110 --> 00:19:04.110
What about QUIC?

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:04.930
You mentioned that.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:05.930
What is QUIC?

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:07.930
ILYA GRIGORIK: So actually,
before I go to QUIC,

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:11.030
can I just make one
more note about SPDY?

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:11.530
MARK: Sure.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:12.655
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Of course.

00:19:12.655 --> 00:19:13.550
MARK: Of course.

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:17.130
ILYA GRIGORIK: SPDY
co-evolved with HTTP/2.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:19.990
SPDY was effectively the
experimental protocol

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:21.050
for HTTP/2.

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:23.040
So while we were
working in HTTP/2 spec,

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.550
we were testing things in SPDY.

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:29.750
And some people have a
good question of-- so

00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:30.600
what does that mean?

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:32.586
Do we keep SPDY, and
do we keep the HTTP/2?

00:19:32.586 --> 00:19:34.210
An the plan there is
to deprecate SPDY.

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.000
So we announced, or
Chrome announced,

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.537
that we will deprecate
SPDY sometime in early 2016

00:19:38.537 --> 00:19:40.120
because we want to
encourage everybody

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:42.090
to shift over to HTTP/2.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:46.890
And for example, when you look
at support for HTTP/2 and SPDY

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:50.720
across some of the new browsers,
like Edge, Microsoft Edge,

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:52.370
they only support HTTP/2 now.

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:55.679
So if you've been running
SPDY, start looking at HTTP/2.

00:19:55.679 --> 00:19:57.220
And the good news
is there's actually

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:58.540
very good service support now.

00:19:58.540 --> 00:20:03.450
Like Nginx and Apache all have
support, App Engine, of course,

00:20:03.450 --> 00:20:07.692
and all the load balancers and
all the rest on Google Cloud

00:20:07.692 --> 00:20:08.400
Platform have it.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.971
So just FYI, if you'be been
running SPDY, look at HTTP/2.

00:20:11.971 --> 00:20:12.470
MARK: No.

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:13.370
That's a very interesting point.

00:20:13.370 --> 00:20:14.911
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's a fair point.

00:20:14.911 --> 00:20:16.640
ILYA GRIGORIK: And
then for QUIC, so this

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:18.950
is an interesting one.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:21.670
Even when we started
working on SPDY,

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:25.040
we knew that the moment you
address issues at the HTTP/2

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.890
layer, you're going to run into
issues at the next layer down,

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:28.656
which is TCP.

00:20:28.656 --> 00:20:33.410
And TCP had some behaviors,
like head of line blocking,

00:20:33.410 --> 00:20:34.790
that are not optimal.

00:20:34.790 --> 00:20:38.580
So for example, if
you have packet loss,

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:40.850
because TCP guarantees
in order delivery,

00:20:40.850 --> 00:20:43.560
if you lose just one packet,
even though you may have other

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.642
packets already
sitting in the buffer,

00:20:45.642 --> 00:20:48.100
you cannot deliver them because
you need to retransmit that

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:50.970
first packet that was dropped
such that you can guarantee

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:52.920
an order delivery.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:55.750
So this seems like, eh, so what?

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:57.930
But in practice, it can
actually be quite bad,

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:01.040
because if you think about
sending 100 requests out,

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:03.352
and then everything is stalled
in that one last packet

00:21:03.352 --> 00:21:04.810
when you could have
been processing

00:21:04.810 --> 00:21:07.580
something else, that's
sub-optimal, so just

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:08.300
one example.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:12.040
Other examples would be
things like mobility or mobile

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:16.100
connections where sometimes
TCP-- or, are not sometimes--

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:21.397
oftentimes, actually, TCP
misunderstands the behavior

00:21:21.397 --> 00:21:23.230
of some of the mobile
networks and considers

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:26.150
it to be like packet loss
where it's really not.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:28.290
So it has sub-optimal behaviors.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:31.200
So looking at all that,
we knew that at some point

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:33.850
we're going to have to dig
a little bit deeper and try

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:38.284
to change TCP or go
some other route.

00:21:38.284 --> 00:21:39.700
But when we start
working on SPDY,

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:43.265
that was frankly a bit
too much to tackle at once

00:21:43.265 --> 00:21:43.890
at both layers.

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:45.790
So we focused on the HTTP layer.

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:50.424
And then as that migrated
towards the ITF working group

00:21:50.424 --> 00:21:52.090
and HTTP where it
kicked off, we started

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:54.230
looking at the next layer down.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.903
So effectively, QUIC
is HTTP/2 over UDP.

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:00.770
MARK: Wow.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:01.440
Interesting.

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.840
ILYA GRIGORIK: Yep.

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:05.910
And you can imagine
how that can be very

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:07.810
interesting and complicated.

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:12.110
So we've tried, and
we do, actually.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:14.410
We have a team
within Google that

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:17.170
works on TCP on Linux
Kernel and other things.

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:20.380
So we've upstreamed many
improvements over time.

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:22.850
But one thing we learned
is that TCP is actually

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:26.472
very slow in terms
of the update cycle,

00:22:26.472 --> 00:22:27.930
getting support
out in the clients,

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:30.849
rolling out a new server, and
all the rest, which is frankly

00:22:30.849 --> 00:22:32.890
for a good reason because
there's so much writing

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:34.880
on it that the
last thing you want

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:37.620
is crazy experiments
running out in the wild

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:40.460
and causing networks
to collapse.

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:44.760
So the people that maintain all
those branches-- and plus you

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:48.530
have all the hardware with
baked-in implementations of it

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:50.640
that you frankly can't
even update at this point.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:54.710
And UDP offers a
very simple interface

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.550
that provides no
primitives whatsoever

00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:59.760
in terms of reliability
and all the rest.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.920
And that's something that
we could experiment with.

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.336
So with that we
started with that.

00:23:03.336 --> 00:23:05.990
And we started
effectively reimplementing

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.290
some of the basic
primitives of TCP,

00:23:08.290 --> 00:23:10.804
but also adding hooks
for new capabilities

00:23:10.804 --> 00:23:13.220
that we could explore, so
things like pluggable congestion

00:23:13.220 --> 00:23:14.230
control.

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:17.260
So maybe we can use different
congestion control strategy

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:20.960
for mobile networks
or 2G networks

00:23:20.960 --> 00:23:26.492
then when you're on Google Fiber
and you have a gigabit link.

00:23:26.492 --> 00:23:28.450
Those have very, very
different characteristics

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:30.140
in terms of how
we want to handle

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:32.430
congestion and other things.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:38.746
We also spent a lot of time
looking at eliminating latency

00:23:38.746 --> 00:23:39.620
during the handshake.

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:43.370
So we talked about
requiring TLS for HTTP/2.

00:23:43.370 --> 00:23:45.780
It turns out that
once you deploy

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:50.580
HTTP/2, because you don't
need the extra connections,

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:53.353
it actually mitigates a lot
of the latency costs of TLS.

00:23:53.353 --> 00:23:56.895
So TLS handshake
itself adds two round

00:23:56.895 --> 00:23:59.860
trips of latency
for the handshake

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:01.990
if it's not optimized.

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:04.340
And if you've
optimized it well, it's

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:08.890
another round trip, which
sometimes can be significant.

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.272
But that latency
is offset by HTTP/2

00:24:12.272 --> 00:24:14.230
because it does not
require as many connections

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:18.180
and you amortize that cost over
the lifetime of the connection.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:21.510
With QUIC, we actually
wanted to get to a zero RTT

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:25.220
handshake, a secure
handshake, where

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.900
we can guarantee that
it's always encrypted,

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:30.370
but we can send application
data in the first round trip.

00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:32.570
So I can just start
talking to the server

00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:35.660
and immediately send
unencrypted data such

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:37.390
that there is no
penalty cost whatsoever

00:24:37.390 --> 00:24:39.700
for using encrypted sections.

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:42.500
So that was another big change.

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:47.900
And this work has been going
on for a while within Chrome.

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:52.010
I think we're on version
23 or 24 of QUIC protocol.

00:24:52.010 --> 00:24:54.260
Some of those changes, of
course, smaller than others.

00:24:54.260 --> 00:25:01.980
But I think it speaks volumes
that we are on version 25,

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:06.520
and in the meantime, 0 new
versions of TCP have shipped.

00:25:06.520 --> 00:25:10.740
MARK: So how much faster
is QUIC than HTTP/2?

00:25:10.740 --> 00:25:12.240
Obviously, it seems
to be determined

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:13.390
in certain situations.

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:16.850
But ballparkish?

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:18.350
ILYA GRIGORIK: So
it really depends.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.830
Actually, the same
question applies to HTTP/2.

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:23.500
How much faster is
HTTP/2 than HTTP/1?

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:24.920
And the answer
there is it really

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.086
depends on your application.

00:25:26.086 --> 00:25:27.800
It depends on where
your users are

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:30.030
based on the characteristics
and all the rest.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:31.480
We do have some
numbers that we've

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:34.140
shared for some of
the Google products.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:35.790
I think this is data
from maybe a year

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:38.630
or so ago where we've
seen improvements

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:41.800
for anywhere between
30% and 50% in terms

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:45.490
of latency, overall latency
for our own products.

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:49.300
Now, if all you do is
you take an HTTP/2 server

00:25:49.300 --> 00:25:53.050
and replace a current server
with an HTTP/2 server,

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:54.820
I can't guarantee
any sort of like,

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.430
oh, it will go two times faster.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.730
It really depends on how well
your server is implemented

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:02.540
and how you're currently
serving your application.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:04.240
How many third
parties do you have?

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:06.060
Are you concatenating
your files?

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:07.760
Are they unbundled
and all the rest?

00:26:07.760 --> 00:26:09.860
So there's a lot
of gotchas in here.

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:13.130
And then to answer
your question on QUIC,

00:26:13.130 --> 00:26:15.710
when we compare it to
HTTP/2, once again,

00:26:15.710 --> 00:26:19.420
it really depends on the
scenarios that we look at.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:22.415
The areas where we found
most success so far

00:26:22.415 --> 00:26:26.470
are actually in some of
the developing markets,

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:28.460
like India, where we
have very slow users

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:30.230
with very slow connections.

00:26:30.230 --> 00:26:33.050
And we've shared some numbers.

00:26:33.050 --> 00:26:37.490
And I can give you guys the
links to the Chromium blog

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:40.140
that has more details on this.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:43.700
For example, YouTube saw
a significant improvement

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:47.200
in the latency, reducing
the number of rebuffers,

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:51.239
and improvement somewhere in
the range of 20% to 30% again

00:26:51.239 --> 00:26:53.030
for the latency of
those users because they

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:56.030
were able to eliminate some of
those retransmissions, delays,

00:26:56.030 --> 00:26:58.570
and other things.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:01.780
So there's no one number that
I can give you that like, oh,

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:03.950
QUIC is X percent faster.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:04.450
MARK: What?

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:05.805
No silver bullet?

00:27:05.805 --> 00:27:06.680
ILYA GRIGORIK: Right.

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:08.055
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
No, but at least

00:27:08.055 --> 00:27:10.730
that's very interesting
that in the connections are

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:12.340
the slowest, that's
where we're going

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.280
to get the best improvement.

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:15.807
That's really interesting.

00:27:15.807 --> 00:27:16.890
ILYA GRIGORIK: Well, yeah.

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:22.040
So at a very high level, the
benefits that we're seeing,

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:25.020
you can think of it as kind
of a long tail distribution

00:27:25.020 --> 00:27:26.614
for a lot of the
connections in terms

00:27:26.614 --> 00:27:27.780
of latency and all the rest.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:29.510
And what QUIC really
helps us with,

00:27:29.510 --> 00:27:31.980
at least based on our
current observations,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.880
is to rein in those
sales and chop them off

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:37.880
because we can explicitly
guard for those things

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:40.910
and implement smarter behaviors
for bigger, slower networks.

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:43.600
So it allows us to be much
more-- to just eliminate

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:46.900
a class of really bad
behaviors, which is actually

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:48.420
very important for
a lot of people

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:50.243
and for a lot of applications.

00:27:50.243 --> 00:27:51.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:52.630
MARK: No, that's good.

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:55.640
So just to be clear as
well, so Google is currently

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.540
experimenting with
QUIC on the GFEs

00:27:58.540 --> 00:27:59.950
and on Google Cloud Platform.

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:02.062
Is that something that's
being experimented on,

00:28:02.062 --> 00:28:03.520
I'm guessing within
Chrome as well?

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:04.170
ILYA GRIGORIK: That's right.

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:04.670
Yeah.

00:28:04.670 --> 00:28:07.380
So we have an
implementation in Chrome.

00:28:07.380 --> 00:28:09.090
And if you're curious,
you can actually

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:13.590
just pull up the Chromium
source and look at the code.

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.490
There's a toy server
in there as well

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:18.120
that I think was just
built for small testing.

00:28:18.120 --> 00:28:21.730
And then our GFE, again,
also implements QUIC,

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:23.170
just as we did with SPDY.

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:25.580
So most of the SPDY-- sorry.

00:28:25.580 --> 00:28:30.050
Most of the Google services
are already speaking QUIC.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.180
One thing that you could
do-- and I recommend

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:36.140
doing this-- is you can
go to the Chrome store

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:38.840
and search for--
what's it called?

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:41.436
I think it's the SPDY Extension.

00:28:41.436 --> 00:28:43.310
MARK: Yeah, the plug-in
that shows the stuff.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.510
ILYA GRIGORIK: And
what it'll do is

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:47.430
it'll show you like
a little lightning

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:49.600
bolt in your navigation bar.

00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.440
It'll show green when you're
talking to a server that's

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:55.759
HTTP/2, and I'll show red when
it's talking to a QUIC server.

00:28:55.759 --> 00:28:57.300
And if you install
that and you start

00:28:57.300 --> 00:28:59.133
navigating to your
favorite Google products,

00:28:59.133 --> 00:29:02.440
you will very quickly find that
it's showing the red bolt quite

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:03.010
often.

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:07.320
And that's because most of
our services like YouTube

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.660
are already being
served over UDP.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.830
So if you're watching
this or something else

00:29:11.830 --> 00:29:14.040
on YouTube in Chrome,
chances are it's actually

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:15.660
talking UDP today.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:16.160
MARK: Yeah.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:16.370
Yeah.

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:17.130
I've got that installed.

00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.850
It's actually really fun
to go around the internet

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:19.680
and have a look.

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:20.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm
installing it right now.

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:21.192
That sounds very interesting.

00:29:21.192 --> 00:29:21.530
MARK: That's really great.

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:22.363
ILYA GRIGORIK: Yeah.

00:29:22.363 --> 00:29:26.890
So speaking of not advertising
these things too much,

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:33.210
I think the same will happen if
you open your App Engine app.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:36.100
And that's running over HTTPS.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:39.900
You'll probably see a
red bolt, which tells you

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.470
that your application
is being served

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:45.660
over QUIC for the
browsers that support it.

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:47.494
MARK: Yes, for the
browsers that support it.

00:29:47.494 --> 00:29:47.993
Yeah.

00:29:47.993 --> 00:29:49.110
I think I remember this.

00:29:49.110 --> 00:29:51.568
And I think I had to tweak a
couple of things inside Chrome

00:29:51.568 --> 00:29:54.660
to get QUIC enabled in the build
that I was running a while ago.

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I just
tried it with normal Chrome,

00:29:57.560 --> 00:30:01.180
our own page, which is
hosted by App Engine.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:03.150
And I get HTTP/2.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:05.189
And we didn't do anything
to it, so that's nice.

00:30:05.189 --> 00:30:05.980
MARK: That is nice.

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:06.771
ILYA GRIGORIK: Yep.

00:30:06.771 --> 00:30:09.750
And so QUIC is already
enabled by default in Chrome.

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:10.780
We do run experiments.

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:14.390
We always have a
group, a random group

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:17.730
that we hold back just for
AB testing and other things.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:20.730
So it may be the case
that if you try to open it

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:24.410
and you don't see it, you
may be in that lucky group.

00:30:24.410 --> 00:30:25.850
So you can force it.

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:28.560
And you can override it if
you go into Chrome settings.

00:30:28.560 --> 00:30:30.324
But just FYI.

00:30:30.324 --> 00:30:30.990
MARK: Wonderful.

00:30:30.990 --> 00:30:31.489
All right.

00:30:31.489 --> 00:30:33.620
Well, before we finish
up, Ilya, is there

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:36.750
anything else you want
to mention, or plug,

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:40.120
or anything like that?

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:41.820
ILYA GRIGORIK: Well,
a couple of things.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:44.510
First off, if you're
running on App Engine,

00:30:44.510 --> 00:30:47.190
definitely go and
check out the blog

00:30:47.190 --> 00:30:51.040
for-- that covers HTTP/2
and that whole flow

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:56.360
of which service are you using
and how do you enable HTTP/2.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:58.140
Start with enabling
HTTPS, and make sure

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:00.630
that you have the
other things enabled.

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:04.390
Watch out for the
server push support.

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:05.939
I think that's coming soon.

00:31:05.939 --> 00:31:07.480
It's actually already
there, I think.

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:09.890
We just haven't really
talked about it.

00:31:09.890 --> 00:31:11.500
I think there's a theme here.

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:13.630
And then finally,
I think there's

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:17.350
QUIC, which lots of people
are really excited about.

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:21.050
So if protocol's something
that you get excited about,

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:25.960
then I definitely encourage you
to go read the draft on ATF.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:29.490
Give us feedback, install
the extension, play with it.

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:31.500
And of course, if you're
running on App Engine,

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:36.754
then poke around and see
how it works under the hood.

00:31:36.754 --> 00:31:37.420
MARK: Wonderful.

00:31:37.420 --> 00:31:37.600
All right.

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:39.430
Well, thank you very much
for joining us today, Ilya.

00:31:39.430 --> 00:31:40.580
That was very informative.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:41.780
I really enjoyed that.

00:31:41.780 --> 00:31:42.905
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:31:42.905 --> 00:31:44.260
I really learned a lot today.

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:45.218
ILYA GRIGORIK: Awesome.

00:31:45.218 --> 00:31:46.050
Thank you, guys.

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.630
MARK: Thank you.

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:49.850
Thanks so much for joining us.

00:31:49.850 --> 00:31:52.590
But we have a wonderful
question of the week.

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:54.680
I've seen this come
up a few times,

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:57.670
people looking at different
solutions for, how

00:31:57.670 --> 00:32:01.880
do you do long running jobs,
like say a video transcoding,

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:03.755
image processing, maybe
even code compilation

00:32:03.755 --> 00:32:05.400
for build systems?

00:32:05.400 --> 00:32:07.650
And what options do they
have available to them

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:10.300
to do that sort of stuff
on Google Cloud Platform,

00:32:10.300 --> 00:32:12.240
but in a cost efficient way?

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:13.924
There should be something there.

00:32:13.924 --> 00:32:14.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:18.620
So the obvious way to do
it would be, of course,

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.110
to just have a machine that
it's running all the time.

00:32:21.110 --> 00:32:23.070
But the problem is
that maybe that's not

00:32:23.070 --> 00:32:25.990
the most cost efficient way.

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:27.620
So if you have
something that you

00:32:27.620 --> 00:32:30.110
can divide into
small chunks of work,

00:32:30.110 --> 00:32:32.750
that you can retry
if something fails,

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:35.330
then let's imagine that
you put those tasks

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:38.370
in some queuing system,
like Pub/Sub for instance--

00:32:38.370 --> 00:32:40.470
MARK: Pub/Sub would
be a great fit.

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:41.980
SPEAKER 1: And then
what you can do

00:32:41.980 --> 00:32:45.990
is have a bunch of
preemptible machines.

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:48.480
And preemptible
VMs, basically what

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:51.860
it means is that those
machines could be preempted,

00:32:51.860 --> 00:32:54.720
or in other words,
you could be asked

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:57.360
to stop using those
machines at some point.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:01.110
But if you can retry
that task later on,

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:02.960
then that's not
really a big issue.

00:33:02.960 --> 00:33:07.460
And now the cool thing is that
the cost is much, much lower.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:09.484
MARK: Preemptible VMs
are up to 70% cheaper.

00:33:09.484 --> 00:33:10.400
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:12.134
So that's a huge thing.

00:33:12.134 --> 00:33:13.300
MARK: That's a good savings.

00:33:13.300 --> 00:33:14.216
That's a good savings.

00:33:14.216 --> 00:33:16.110
And it's pretty much
a regular machine.

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:19.100
They can last for up to 24
hours, which is really cool.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:19.600
But yeah.

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:21.891
If they fall over, and you're
able to just be like, OK.

00:33:21.891 --> 00:33:22.510
That's fine.

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:24.100
We'll just try again
for this thing.

00:33:24.100 --> 00:33:26.862
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And anyway,
if you're writing anything

00:33:26.862 --> 00:33:28.320
that it's running
on the cloud, you

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:32.040
should always be aware of the
machine could fail at any time.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:34.080
So basically, most
of the time you

00:33:34.080 --> 00:33:37.480
don't need to add anything that
you had not been doing before.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:37.980
MARK: Yeah.

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.438
I can totally see
this makes sense,

00:33:39.438 --> 00:33:42.990
Pub/Sub with a preemptible
VM, pulling stuff off.

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:44.520
That makes so much sense.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:46.530
And it could give you
some really good savings.

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:47.446
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:47.446 --> 00:33:49.240
And actually, if
some of you might

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:51.860
have heard about this
really cool thing

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:54.860
that Google created,
MapReduce, which

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:59.470
is just a more sophisticated way
of doing exactly the same idea,

00:33:59.470 --> 00:34:01.920
doing just a bunch of
jobs that could fail,

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:03.420
and they will be
retried and so on.

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:04.930
But it's much more complicated.

00:34:04.930 --> 00:34:08.739
Running MapReduce
on preemptible VMs

00:34:08.739 --> 00:34:11.460
is a great match,
because MapReduce

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:14.120
will be managing the
fact that if job fails,

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:15.550
there will be trial run.

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:17.219
So you don't need to
add anything else.

00:34:17.219 --> 00:34:19.489
Just say, for
instance, maybe you

00:34:19.489 --> 00:34:21.540
will have an instance group.

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:23.190
And that instance
then played, it

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:25.500
will say that VM
should be preemtible.

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:26.310
That's it.

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:30.730
Just because you say
that, you get huge saving.

00:34:30.730 --> 00:34:32.440
MARK: You can do also
really cool stuff.

00:34:32.440 --> 00:34:33.607
All our stuff is API driven.

00:34:33.607 --> 00:34:35.356
So if you want to spin
these out with APIs

00:34:35.356 --> 00:34:37.962
and manage them yourselves, you
can totally do that as well.

00:34:37.962 --> 00:34:38.920
I've seen people do it.

00:34:38.920 --> 00:34:40.350
It works really, really well.

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:42.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
lots of cool things

00:34:42.350 --> 00:34:48.170
that you can do with preemptible
VMs, lower cost, and still

00:34:48.170 --> 00:34:51.460
the same performance, just
if might be preemptive.

00:34:51.460 --> 00:34:52.260
MARK: Wonderful.

